{"kind":"FETCH","data":{"headers":{},"body":"{\"id\":13712845,\"site\":\"lawnsguide\",\"type\":\"lawn-care\",\"short_title\":\"lawn-care-schedule-northeast\",\"language\":\"en\",\"published_time\":\"2026-08-26 00:23:04\",\"modified_time\":\"2026-08-26 00:23:04\",\"author\":\"Emily Watson\",\"img\":\"https://s.alicdn.com/@sc02/kf/A46ee71f639f544d5947e575d76ef530d4.jpg\",\"title\":\"Northeast Lawn Care Schedule: A Complete Season-by-Season Guide\",\"description\":\"Master your Northeast lawn care schedule with our month-by-month guide covering fertilizing, mowing, aeration, overseeding & seasonal tips for a lush yard.\",\"url\":\"/lawn-care/lawn-care-schedule-northeast\",\"body\":\"<h1>Northeast Lawn Care Schedule: Your Complete Season-by-Season Guide</h1>\\n\\n<p>If you live in the Northeast, you already know that maintaining a healthy, green lawn is no small feat. The region&rsquo;s climate&mdash;characterized by cold winters, humid summers, and unpredictable spring weather&mdash;demands a tailored approach to lawn care that differs significantly from schedules used in the South or Midwest. Cool-season grasses like <a href=\\\"https://lawnsguide.com/grass-types/kentucky-bluegrass\\\">Kentucky bluegrass</a>, <a href=\\\"https://lawnsguide.com/grass-types/perennial-ryegrass\\\">perennial ryegrass</a>, and <a href=\\\"https://lawnsguide.com/grass-types/fine-fescue\\\">fine fescue</a> dominate Northeastern lawns, and each one has specific needs throughout the year.</p>\\n\\n<p>This comprehensive Northeast lawn care schedule breaks down exactly what you need to do&mdash;and when&mdash;to keep your turf thick, resilient, and beautiful. Whether you&rsquo;re a first-time homeowner in Massachusetts or a seasoned gardener in Pennsylvania, this guide gives you a practical, actionable roadmap for every season. Bookmark this page and refer back to it throughout the year so you never miss a critical lawn care window.</p>\\n\\n<h2>Early Spring Lawn Care (March &ndash; April): Waking Up Your Lawn</h2>\\n\\n<p>Spring in the Northeast arrives slowly. Ground temperatures remain cold well into March, and the last frost can hit as late as May in some areas. The key during early spring is patience&mdash;pushing your lawn too hard too early can cause more harm than good. Here&rsquo;s what you should focus on during these transitional months.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Assess Winter Damage</h3>\\n\\n<p>Before doing anything else, walk your entire lawn and look for signs of winter damage. Snow mold, salt damage from road treatment, and compacted areas from heavy snow accumulation are all common issues in the Northeast. Rake away dead grass and debris from affected areas to allow sunlight and air to reach the soil surface. For more details on identifying and treating these problems, check out our guide on <a href=\\\"https://lawnsguide.com/lawn-care/common-lawn-diseases\\\">common lawn diseases</a>.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Wait to Fertilize</h3>\\n\\n<p>One of the most common mistakes Northeast homeowners make is fertilizing too early. Cool-season grasses are still semi-dormant when soil temperatures are below 50&deg;F. Applying fertilizer before the grass is actively growing wastes product and can promote weed growth instead. Wait until soil temperatures consistently reach 50&ndash;55&deg;F, which typically happens in mid-to-late April across most of the Northeast. A simple soil thermometer is an invaluable tool for this.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Apply Pre-Emergent Herbicide</h3>\\n\\n<p>Timing is critical when it comes to pre-emergent herbicide application. The goal is to create a chemical barrier in the soil <em>before</em> crabgrass and other annual weeds germinate. In the Northeast, this window usually falls between mid-April and early May, depending on your specific location. A good natural indicator: apply pre-emergent when forsythia bushes finish blooming. Products containing prodiamine or dithiopyr are effective choices for cool-season lawns.</p>\\n\\n<p><strong>Recommended tools &amp; products for early spring:</strong></p>\\n<ul>\\n<li>Soil thermometer for monitoring ground temperature</li>\\n<li>Thatching rake or power rake for removing dead grass</li>\\n<li>Pre-emergent herbicide (prodiamine-based for long-lasting control)</li>\\n<li>Broadcast spreader for even application</li>\\n</ul>\\n\\n<h3>Begin Mowing When Ready</h3>\\n\\n<p>Your first mow of the season should happen when the grass reaches about 3 to 3.5 inches in height. Set your mower blade to remove no more than one-third of the grass blade length. Make sure your mower blades are sharp&mdash;dull blades tear the grass rather than cutting it cleanly, which opens the door to disease. Visit our <a href=\\\"https://lawnsguide.com/lawn-care/mowing-tips\\\">mowing tips guide</a> for a complete breakdown of proper mowing techniques.</p>\\n\\n<h2>Late Spring &amp; Early Summer (May &ndash; June): Building a Strong Foundation</h2>\\n\\n<p>By May, your lawn is actively growing and this is the period where you set the foundation for a healthy summer. The decisions you make during late spring directly impact how well your grass handles the heat and drought stress that inevitably arrives in July and August.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Apply Spring Fertilizer</h3>\\n\\n<p>Once your lawn is actively growing and you&rsquo;ve mowed it two or three times, it&rsquo;s time for your first real fertilizer application of the year. Use a slow-release, granular fertilizer with a balanced NPK ratio such as 20-5-10 or 24-4-12. Slow-release formulas feed the grass gradually over six to eight weeks, which prevents the surge of growth that can weaken the plant heading into summer heat. Apply at a rate of approximately 1 pound of nitrogen per 1,000 square feet.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Overseed Bare or Thin Spots</h3>\\n\\n<p>Early to mid-May is one of the two prime overseeding windows in the Northeast (the other being early fall). If you have bare patches from winter damage or thin areas from the previous season, now is the time to address them. Choose a seed blend that matches your existing lawn. For most Northeast lawns, a mix of Kentucky bluegrass and perennial ryegrass provides excellent results. Prepare the soil by loosening the top layer, spread seed at the recommended rate, and keep the area consistently moist until seedlings are established. Read our full <a href=\\\"https://lawnsguide.com/lawn-care/overseeding-guide\\\">overseeding guide</a> for step-by-step instructions.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Control Broadleaf Weeds</h3>\\n\\n<p>Dandelions, clover, and plantain are aggressive in the Northeast during late spring. Spot-treat broadleaf weeds with a selective herbicide labeled for use on cool-season grasses. Avoid blanket applications if possible&mdash;targeted treatment protects your grass while eliminating the weeds. Be sure not to apply broadleaf herbicides within three days of overseeding, as they can inhibit seed germination.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Adjust Mowing Height</h3>\\n\\n<p>As temperatures begin to climb in June, gradually raise your mower deck to a cutting height of 3 to 4 inches. Taller grass shades the soil, which reduces water evaporation, suppresses weed germination, and encourages deeper root growth. This single adjustment is one of the most impactful things you can do for your lawn&rsquo;s summer survival. For more on optimal mowing heights for different grass types, see our <a href=\\\"https://lawnsguide.com/grass-types/cool-season-grasses\\\">cool-season grass guide</a>.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Begin Grub Prevention</h3>\\n\\n<p>Japanese beetles, European chafers, and June bugs are prevalent across the Northeast, and their larvae&mdash;grubs&mdash;can devastate a lawn by feeding on grassroots beneath the surface. Apply a preventive grub control product containing chlorantraniliprole or imidacloprid in late May through June. These products work systemically and provide protection through the peak grub feeding period in late summer and early fall.</p>\\n\\n<p><strong>Recommended tools &amp; products for late spring:</strong></p>\\n<ul>\\n<li>Slow-release granular fertilizer (20-5-10 or similar)</li>\\n<li>Quality grass seed blend matched to your lawn</li>\\n<li>Selective broadleaf herbicide for spot treatment</li>\\n<li>Preventive grub control product</li>\\n<li>Oscillating or impact sprinkler for supplemental watering</li>\\n</ul>\\n\\n<h2>Summer Lawn Care (July &ndash; August): Survival Mode</h2>\\n\\n<p>Summer is the most stressful season for cool-season lawns in the Northeast. High temperatures, humidity, and periodic drought all conspire against your grass. The overarching strategy for summer lawn care is defensive: protect the grass you have, minimize stress, and avoid aggressive treatments that could push an already-struggling lawn over the edge.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Water Deeply &amp; Infrequently</h3>\\n\\n<p>Your lawn needs approximately 1 to 1.5 inches of water per week during summer, whether from rainfall or irrigation. The best approach is deep, infrequent watering&mdash;about 30 to 45 minutes per zone, two to three times per week&mdash;rather than light daily sprinklings. Deep watering encourages roots to grow downward in search of moisture, which makes the entire plant more drought-resistant. Water in the early morning (between 5 a.m. and 9 a.m.) to minimize evaporation and reduce the risk of fungal diseases that thrive on wet grass overnight.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Raise Mowing Height &amp; Reduce Frequency</h3>\\n\\n<p>During peak summer heat, raise your mower deck to 3.5 or even 4 inches. Taller grass blades photosynthesize more efficiently, shade the soil surface, and keep root zones cooler. You may also want to mow slightly less frequently during heat waves. If growth slows due to drought or extreme heat, let the grass grow a bit longer rather than forcing a mow. Never mow during the hottest part of the day&mdash;early evening is the best time during summer.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Watch for Fungal Diseases</h3>\\n\\n<p>The combination of heat and humidity in the Northeast creates ideal conditions for lawn diseases like brown patch, dollar spot, and powdery mildew. Brown patch appears as irregular, tan-colored patches that can spread rapidly during warm, wet periods. If you spot signs of disease, reduce watering frequency (but not total volume), improve air circulation by trimming surrounding vegetation, and apply a fungicide if the problem persists. Our <a href=\\\"https://lawnsguide.com/lawn-care/lawn-fungus-treatment\\\">lawn fungus treatment guide</a> covers identification and solutions in detail.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Avoid Fertilizing During Heat Stress</h3>\\n\\n<p>Do not apply nitrogen-heavy fertilizer during July and August. Pushing growth during heat stress forces the grass to expend energy it doesn&rsquo;t have, which weakens the root system and increases susceptibility to disease and drought damage. If your lawn shows signs of nutrient deficiency (such as pale yellow color), a light application of iron can green up the turf without stimulating excessive top growth.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Manage Chinch Bugs &amp; Sod Webworms</h3>\\n\\n<p>While grubs are the most well-known lawn pest in the Northeast, chinch bugs and sod webworms can also cause significant damage during summer. Chinch bugs are particularly problematic in sunny, hot areas of the lawn. Look for yellowing patches that don&rsquo;t respond to watering. A simple flotation test&mdash;using a coffee can filled with water pressed into the soil&mdash;can reveal their presence. Treat with an appropriate insecticide if populations are high enough to warrant action.</p>\\n\\n<p><strong>Recommended tools &amp; products for summer:</strong></p>\\n<ul>\\n<li>Programmable sprinkler timer for consistent early-morning watering</li>\\n<li>Rain gauge to track total weekly water accumulation</li>\\n<li>Preventive fungicide (for lawns with a history of disease)</li>\\n<li>Iron supplement for mid-summer color boost without growth surge</li>\\n<li>Sharp mower blades (always keep a spare set on hand)</li>\\n</ul>\\n\\n<h2>Fall Lawn Care (September &ndash; November): The Most Important Season</h2>\\n\\n<p>If you only have time to invest heavily in one season of lawn care, make it fall. The combination of warm soil, cool air temperatures, and increased rainfall creates the ideal environment for cool-season grass recovery and establishment. Every task you complete in fall pays dividends the following spring. Many lawn care professionals consider September the single most important month for Northeast lawns.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Core Aeration</h3>\\n\\n<p>Core aeration involves removing small plugs of soil from the lawn to relieve compaction, improve water infiltration, and allow oxygen to reach the root zone. In the Northeast, where clay-heavy soils are common, annual aeration is often essential. The best time to aerate is early September, when the grass is entering its peak fall growth period and can recover quickly. Rent a core aerator from a local equipment rental center or hire a professional lawn service. Leave the soil plugs on the lawn&mdash;they will break down naturally and return beneficial microorganisms to the surface. For a deeper dive, read our complete <a href=\\\"https://lawnsguide.com/lawn-care/lawn-aeration-guide\\\">lawn aeration guide</a>.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Fall Overseeding</h3>\\n\\n<p>Early September through mid-October is the prime overseeding window in the Northeast. The soil is still warm enough to promote rapid germination, and cooler air temperatures reduce stress on young seedlings. Overseeding immediately after aeration gives seed direct soil contact, dramatically improving germination rates. Use a high-quality seed blend appropriate for your site conditions&mdash;shade-tolerant fine fescue blends for wooded lots, or aggressive Kentucky bluegrass blends for sunny, high-traffic areas. Keep newly seeded areas consistently moist for the first two to three weeks.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Heavy Fall Fertilization</h3>\\n\\n<p>Fall fertilization is arguably the single most important nutrient application of the year for Northeast lawns. Apply a high-nitrogen, slow-release fertilizer in early September (around Labor Day) at a rate of 1 to 1.5 pounds of nitrogen per 1,000 square feet. This application fuels strong root development and carbohydrate storage that carries the grass through winter. A second, lighter application in late October or early November&mdash;often called a &ldquo;winterizer&rdquo; fertilizer&mdash;with a higher potassium content helps the grass harden off and improves cold tolerance. Look for a formulation like 24-0-10 or 18-0-12 for this late-fall application.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Manage Fallen Leaves</h3>\\n\\n<p>The Northeast&rsquo;s beautiful autumn foliage is also one of its biggest lawn care challenges. A thick layer of leaves blocks sunlight, traps moisture against the grass, and creates an environment ripe for snow mold development. Rake or mulch leaves regularly throughout October and November. Mulching mowers can chop leaves into fine pieces that decompose quickly and return nutrients to the soil, but avoid letting leaf fragments accumulate so thickly that they smother the grass. If you have heavy tree cover, you may need to mulch or rake weekly during peak leaf drop.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Final Mowing &amp; Winterization</h3>\\n\\n<p>Continue mowing as long as the grass is growing, which in many parts of the Northeast extends into late November. For your final mow of the season, lower the cutting height gradually to about 2.5 inches. Shorter grass going into winter is less prone to snow mold and reduces habitat for voles that tunnel beneath the snow. After the last mow, drain and store your irrigation system, clean and service your mower, and sharpen blades so you&rsquo;re ready for spring. Our <a href=\\\"https://lawnsguide.com/lawn-care/winterizing-your-lawn\\\">winterizing guide</a> covers end-of-season preparation in detail.</p>\\n\\n<p><strong>Recommended tools &amp; products for fall:</strong></p>\\n<ul>\\n<li>Core aerator (rental or professional service)</li>\\n<li>High-quality grass seed blend for overseeding</li>\\n<li>Slow-release fall fertilizer (high nitrogen for September application)</li>\\n<li>Winterizer fertilizer (higher potassium for November application)</li>\\n<li>Mulching mower or leaf blower/vacuum for leaf management</li>\\n<li>Starter fertilizer for newly seeded areas</li>\\n</ul>\\n\\n<h2>Winter Lawn Care (December &ndash; February): Rest &amp; Preparation</h2>\\n\\n<p>Your lawn is dormant during the Northeast winter, but that doesn&rsquo;t mean you should ignore it entirely. A few simple practices during the winter months protect your turf and set you up for a strong start when spring arrives.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Minimize Foot Traffic on Dormant Grass</h3>\\n\\n<p>Walking on frozen or snow-covered grass can damage the crown of the plant, especially when frost is present. Designate specific paths through your yard and encourage family members and guests to stick to walkways and driveways. Repeated traffic on dormant turf creates compacted areas that will be visible as thin spots the following spring.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Avoid Salt Damage</h3>\\n\\n<p>Road salt and ice melt products can severely damage lawn edges along driveways, sidewalks, and streets. Sodium chloride draws moisture out of grass roots and alters soil chemistry. Use calcium chloride or magnesium chloride-based ice melt products near lawn areas whenever possible, as they are less harmful to turf. In early spring, flush salt-affected areas with plenty of water and apply gypsum (calcium sulfate) to help restore soil structure.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Plan &amp; Prepare for Spring</h3>\\n\\n<p>Winter is the perfect time to evaluate the past season&rsquo;s lawn care results and plan improvements. Order soil test kits to send to your local cooperative extension service as soon as the ground thaws. Research new products, review your lawn care schedule, and order seed and fertilizer early to ensure availability. If you&rsquo;re considering hiring a professional lawn care service, winter is when many companies offer early-bird pricing.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Maintain Equipment</h3>\\n\\n<p>Use the off-season to service all your lawn care equipment. Change the oil and air filter on your mower, sharpen or replace blades, clean and oil garden tools, and inspect your sprinkler system for damaged heads or leaks. Well-maintained equipment performs better, lasts longer, and produces superior results. Check out our <a href=\\\"https://lawnsguide.com/lawn-care/lawn-equipment-maintenance\\\">equipment maintenance guide</a> for a complete checklist.</p>\\n\\n<h2>Northeast Lawn Care Calendar: Quick-Reference Month-by-Month Guide</h2>\\n\\n<p>Use this condensed calendar as a quick reference throughout the year. Each month lists the primary tasks you should be completing based on typical Northeast climate patterns.</p>\\n\\n<table>\\n<thead>\\n<tr>\\n<th>Month</th>\\n<th>Primary Tasks</th>\\n</tr>\\n</thead>\\n<tbody>\\n<tr>\\n<td>March</td>\\n<td>Assess winter damage, clear debris, plan spring tasks</td>\\n</tr>\\n<tr>\\n<td>April</td>\\n<td>Apply pre-emergent herbicide, first mow, light raking, soil test</td>\\n</tr>\\n<tr>\\n<td>May</td>\\n<td>Spring fertilizer application, overseed bare spots, begin grub prevention, weed control</td>\\n</tr>\\n<tr>\\n<td>June</td>\\n<td>Raise mowing height, apply grub control, monitor for disease, adjust watering</td>\\n</tr>\\n<tr>\\n<td>July</td>\\n<td>Deep &amp; infrequent watering, monitor for insects &amp; disease, avoid fertilizing</td>\\n</tr>\\n<tr>\\n<td>August</td>\\n<td>Continue watering, prepare for fall renovation, order seed &amp; fertilizer</td>\\n</tr>\\n<tr>\\n<td>September</td>\\n<td>Core aerate, overseed, apply fall fertilizer, repair damaged areas</td>\\n</tr>\\n<tr>\\n<td>October</td>\\n<td>Continue overseeding, manage leaves, apply second fall fertilizer, reduce mowing height</td>\\n</tr>\\n<tr>\\n<td>November</td>\\n<td>Final mow, apply winterizer fertilizer, clean up leaves, winterize equipment</td>\\n</tr>\\n<tr>\\n<td>December&ndash;February</td>\\n<td>Minimize traffic, avoid salt damage, plan for spring, service equipment</td>\\n</tr>\\n</tbody>\\n</table>\\n\\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions About Northeast Lawn Care</h2>\\n\\n<h3>When should I first fertilize my lawn in the Northeast?</h3>\\n\\n<p>The best time for your first fertilizer application is mid-to-late April, once soil temperatures consistently reach 50&ndash;55&deg;F and the grass is actively growing. Applying fertilizer too early wastes product and can stimulate weed growth rather than turf growth. Use a slow-release granular fertilizer and apply at a rate of about 1 pound of nitrogen per 1,000 square feet. A soil test will help you determine the exact nutrient needs of your specific lawn. For more on choosing the right fertilizer, visit our <a href=\\\"https://lawnsguide.com/lawn-care/fertilizer-guide\\\">fertilizer guide</a>.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Is it better to overseed in spring or fall in the Northeast?</h3>\\n\\n<p>Fall is the superior time to overseed in the Northeast, specifically from early September through mid-October. Fall overseeding benefits from warm soil that promotes rapid germination, cool air temperatures that reduce seedling stress, and fewer competing weeds. Spring overseeding (May) is a viable second option but faces challenges from summer heat stress on young seedlings and competition from germinating weeds. If you overseed in spring, avoid applying pre-emergent herbicide to those areas, as it will prevent grass seed germination as well.</p>\\n\\n<h3>How often should I water my Northeast lawn during summer?</h3>\\n\\n<p>Aim for 1 to 1.5 inches of water per week during the growing season, delivered through two or three deep watering sessions rather than daily light sprinklings. Water in the early morning between 5 a.m. and 9 a.m. to minimize evaporation and reduce the risk of fungal disease. Use a rain gauge to track total water accumulation from both rainfall and irrigation. During drought periods, it&rsquo;s acceptable to let cool-season grass go dormant&mdash;it will turn brown but will recover when moisture returns. However, if you choose to water, commit to consistent watering rather than sporadic irrigation, which can do more harm than good.</p>\\n\\n<h3>What is the best grass type for a Northeast lawn?</h3>\\n\\n<p>The best grass types for the Northeast are cool-season grasses, specifically Kentucky bluegrass, perennial ryegrass, tall fescue, and fine fescue. Kentucky bluegrass offers excellent density and self-repairing ability through rhizomes but requires full sun and consistent moisture. Perennial ryegrass germinates quickly and handles traffic well, making it a great component in seed blends. Tall fescue has the deepest root system and best drought tolerance, making it ideal for low-maintenance lawns. Fine fescue excels in shade and low-fertility conditions. Most Northeast lawns benefit from a blend of two or more of these species. Explore our <a href=\\\"https://lawnsguide.com/grass-types/best-grass-for-northeast\\\">best grass types for the Northeast</a> guide for detailed comparisons.</p>\\n\\n<h3>How do I fix salt-damaged lawn edges along my driveway?</h3>\\n\\n<p>Salt damage along driveways and sidewalks is extremely common in the Northeast. In early spring, thoroughly flush the affected areas with water to leach excess sodium from the soil. Apply gypsum (calcium sulfate) at a rate of about 50 pounds per 1,000 square feet&mdash;gypsum replaces sodium ions in the soil with calcium, restoring soil structure. Rake out dead grass, add a thin layer of compost, and overseed with a salt-tolerant grass variety such as tall fescue or perennial ryegrass. To prevent future damage, switch to calcium chloride or magnesium chloride-based ice melt products, which are significantly less harmful to turf than sodium chloride. Installing a physical barrier such as burlap or snow fencing along lawn edges can also reduce salt spray exposure.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Should I dethatch my Northeast lawn, and how often?</h3>\\n\\n<p>Thatch is a layer of dead and living organic matter that accumulates between the soil surface and the green grass blades. A thin layer (less than half an inch) is beneficial&mdash;it insulates the soil and retains moisture. However, when thatch exceeds half an inch, it blocks water, nutrients, and air from reaching the root zone. In the Northeast, Kentucky bluegrass lawns are most prone to thatch buildup due to their aggressive rhizome growth. Check your thatch layer annually by cutting a small wedge of turf with a knife. If dethatching is needed, do it in early fall when the grass can recover quickly. Use a power rake or vertical mower for heavy thatch, or a manual thatching rake for small areas. Core aeration also helps manage thatch by introducing soil microorganisms that break down organic matter. Learn more in our <a href=\\\"https://lawnsguide.com/lawn-care/dethatching-guide\\\">dethatching guide</a>.</p>\\n\\n<h2>Final Thoughts on Your Northeast Lawn Care Schedule</h2>\\n\\n<p>Maintaining a healthy lawn in the Northeast requires working with the natural growth cycles of cool-season grasses rather than against them. The most important takeaway from this schedule is that fall is your lawn&rsquo;s most productive season&mdash;invest your time and resources heavily in aeration, overseeding, and fertilization during September and October, and you&rsquo;ll be rewarded with a thick, resilient lawn the following spring.</p>\\n\\n<p>Remember that every lawn is unique. Factors like soil type, sun exposure, tree cover, and foot traffic all influence which tasks are most important for your specific yard. A soil test is the single best investment you can make&mdash;it removes the guesswork from fertilization and pH management. Contact your local cooperative extension office for affordable, region-specific soil testing services.</p>\\n\\n<p>Stick to this schedule, adjust based on your observations and local conditions, and your Northeast lawn will be the envy of the neighborhood season after season. For more tips and in-depth guides on every aspect of lawn care, explore our full library of <a href=\\\"https://lawnsguide.com/lawn-care\\\">lawn care resources</a> and find the perfect <a href=\\\"https://lawnsguide.com/grass-types\\\">grass type</a> for your region.</p>\",\"tag\":null,\"is_online\":\"Y\",\"subtype\":null,\"slug\":null,\"cover_image\":null,\"status\":\"published\",\"created_at\":\"2026-08-26 00:32:54\"}","status":200,"url":""},"revalidate":3600,"tags":[]}